exercises.solution:
```python
def mean_difference(acts_a, acts_b):
    return acts_a.mean(axis=0) - acts_b.mean(axis=0)

def ablate(X, v):
    v_hat = v / np.linalg.norm(v)
    return X - np.outer(X @ v_hat, v_hat)
```

This is the orthogonal projection onto the complement of $v$: idempotent (applying it twice is the same as once), and it leaves directions orthogonal to $v$ alone. Applied at every layer and position, it is the technique researchers used to remove refusal behaviour from open chat models by ablating a single 'refusal direction' — evidence both for the linear representation hypothesis and for how thin some safety training can be. Adding a multiple of $v$ instead of removing it is activation steering.
